{
  "response": "**VERDICT: REVISE**\n\n**Overall Assessment**: The component demonstrates strong foundational accessibility practices: native `<button>` elements, proper focus indicators, correct external link handling, and a valid text-alternative pattern for the map. However, the core interaction model (map controls updating state in-place) lacks programmatic feedback to assistive technology, creating a significant gap for screen reader users. Touch target sizing leaves minimal margin against WCAG 2.5.8, and the third-party map mount point requires explicit dependency handling to ensure focus management remains coherent. The design is sound but incomplete.\n\n**Pre-commitment Predictions**: \nBefore reading code, I predicted: (1) Map control state changes would not be announced to AT, (2) Text alternative link placement might confuse screen reader users without explicit labeling, (3) Button touch targets could fall below recommended sizing, (4) The map canvas placeholder lacks `role=\"application\"` or focus management delegation. All predictions materialized as design gaps or require explicit handling.\n\n**Critical Findings** (blocks access):\nNone identified. No complete access loss for any user group.\n\n**Major Findings** (significantly degrades experience):\n1. **Map control state changes are not communicated to assistive technology.** \n   - Evidence: `WatershedMapPanel.jsx:6-8` handlers log to console but update no ARIA attributes or live regions. The map canvas (`jsx:14`) is a passive mount point with no programmatic state sync.\n   - User group impacted: Screen reader users\n   - WCAG/APG citation: WCAG 4.1.3 (Status Messages), WAI-ARIA APG Pattern for Interactive Widgets\n   - Expected behavior: When zoom/pan changes, the new state must be announced to AT or reflected in button labels.\n   - Fix: Add a `div aria-live=\"polite\" id=\"map-status\"` inside the panel. Update it on each control click (`document.getElementById('map-status').textContent = 'Zoom level changed to 150%'`). Alternatively, update button text dynamically (e.g., `Zoom in (current: 150%)`) and use `aria-label` for screen readers only.\n   - Confidence: HIGH\n   - Why this matters: Screen reader users will hear \"button, Zoom in\" but receive zero feedback when the map updates. The interaction becomes a black box.\n\n2. **Text alternative link lacks explicit identification as the map's text alternative.**\n   - Evidence: `WatershedMapPanel.jsx:17-19` places a standard link after the map canvas without clarifying its relationship to the graphic.\n   - User group impacted: Screen reader users, cognitive accessibility users\n   - WCAG/APG citation: WCAG 1.1.1 (Non-text Content), WCAG 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value)\n   - Expected behavior: The link must be explicitly labeled as the text alternative to prevent confusion with other navigation or content links.\n   - Fix: Add `aria-label=\"Text alternative for the watershed boundary map\"` to the `<a>`, or wrap it in a `<p aria-describedby=\"map-alt-desc\">` with an explicit description. Example: `<p><a href=\"/watershed/boundaries-table\" aria-label=\"View watershed boundaries as a data table (text alternative)\">View watershed boundaries as a data table</a></p>`\n   - Confidence: HIGH\n   - Why this matters: Without explicit labeling, SR users encounter an unexplained link that appears disconnected from the map. Cognitive users may not understand why they're clicking it.\n\n**Minor Findings** (friction but workaround exists):\n- **Touch target sizing leaves minimal margin against WCAG 2.5.8 minimum.** \n  - Evidence: `map-controls.css:17` applies `padding: 6px 14px`. Combined with icon and gap, button height is ~32px. Passes the 24x24 CSS pixel minimum but falls short of the 44x44 recommended target.\n  - User group impacted: Low vision / motor impairment users\n  - WCAG citation: WCAG 2.5.8 (Target Size)\n  - Fix: Increase padding to `8px 16px` or add `min-height: 44px; min-width: 44px;` to `.map-control`.\n\n- **Text color contrast is passing but leaves zero margin for error.** \n  - Evidence: `#0b5fff` on `#ffffff` yields ~4.53:1 (passes 4.5:1 threshold).\n  - User group impacted: Low vision users\n  - WCAG citation: WCAG 1.4.3 (Contrast Minimum)\n  - Fix: Consider darkening to `#0a4fe6` or similar to provide a safety margin if backgrounds shift during theming or printing.\n\n**Enhancements** (best practice not met):\n- **Map canvas mount point lacks focus management delegation.** If the third-party library renders an interactive map, it likely requires `role=\"application\"` and manages its own focus trap. The host component should either: (a) add `role=\"application\"` to `.map-canvas` and document that the library handles focus, or (b) provide a skip link/focus management hook if the library doesn't.\n- **External link SR hint could be consolidated.** The visually-hidden span is correct but adds DOM noise. Consider using `aria-label=\"Open full report (opens in a new tab)\"` on the `<a>` instead of a separate span, reducing markup complexity.\n\n**What's Missing** (gaps, unhandled edge cases, unstated assumptions):\n- **Missing live region for dynamic map state**: Controls update visual state but provide no programmatic feedback loop.\n- **Missing focus management contract for third-party mount**: No documentation or ARIA attributes clarifying who owns focus when the map renders.\n- **Missing aria-describedby on map canvas**: If the map has a title/description, it should be associated via `aria-describedby` pointing to the `<h2>` or a separate description element.\n\n**Multi-Perspective Notes**:\n| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal |\n|-------------|-------------|----------------|\n| Screen reader | HIGH | State changes unannounced; text alternative link context unclear |\n| Keyboard-only | LOW | Native buttons handle focus naturally; tab order matches visual |\n| Low vision | MEDIUM | Touch targets near minimum; contrast passes but tight; focus indicators meet WCAG 2.4.13 |\n| Cognitive | MEDIUM | Clear labels and external link hint present, but map state feedback gap creates uncertainty |\n| Vestibular & motion | LOW | No animations or auto-playing content in component |\n| Auditory access | LOW | No audio/video elements |\n| Environmental contrast | LOW | Focus indicators meet threshold; color not sole indicator of state |\n\n**Verdict Justification**: The component passes automated checks and demonstrates correct native HTML usage, landmark structure, and focus indicator design. However, the core interaction model fails to communicate state changes to assistive technology, which is a fundamental design gap for screen reader users. This is not a compliance violation but a coherent interaction model failure. The text alternative pattern is valid but needs explicit labeling to function correctly in AT. Touch targets and contrast are technically compliant but leave insufficient margin for real-world usage. **REVISE** is required to add state feedback, clarify the text alternative relationship, and harden touch target sizing before shipping.\n\n**Open Questions (unscored)**:\n- Does the third-party map library automatically announce zoom/pan changes via its own live regions? If yes, the host component's gap is mitigated but should still be verified.\n- Is the map canvas expected to receive keyboard focus directly, or will the library handle internal navigation? If external, `role=\"application\"` and focus delegation are required.\n- Will the button colors change in dark mode or high contrast mode? The current blue-on-white contrast leaves no safety margin for theme shifts.\n\n```\n### A11y Evidence Finding\nfinding_id: map-state-feedback-missing\nfingerprint: 8a3f9c2e1d4b7065\nsource: WatershedMapPanel.jsx:6-8, jsx:14\nwcag_or_apg: WCAG 4.1.3 (Status Messages), WAI-ARIA APG Interactive Widgets\nsection_508_fpc_context: not in scope\nseverity: MAJOR\nperspective_alarms: screen reader=HIGH, cognitive=MEDIUM\nevidence: JSX handlers log to console only; no aria-live region or button label updates present. Map canvas div has no role/aria-describedby.\nreproduction_steps: 1. Mount WatershedMapPanel in a browser with NVDA/VoiceOver. 2. Click \"Zoom in\" button. 3. Observe screen reader output.\nexpected_behavior: Screen reader announces zoom level change or button label updates to reflect new state.\nactual_behavior: No programmatic feedback is announced; user hears only the button activation sound.\ntrend: new\n```",
